lls","poi_name":"Big Falls","subject":"","author":"Brandon Hughett","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/41295","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/41295"}},{"id":"786341","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"786330","description":"","caption":"Tuck tight","poi_name":"Compression Falls","subject":"Chris Grindstaff","author":"Edgar Peck","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/786341","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/786341"}},{"id":"7108","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7135","description":"","caption":"In the falls","poi_name":"Big Falls","subject":"Caleb Mentzer","author":"Nick Scott","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/7108","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/7108"}},{"id":"786338","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"786330","description":"Portage is narrow, but not unreasonable. ","caption":"","poi_name":"Twisting Falls Portage","subject":"Edgar Peck, Chris Grindstaff","author":"Trevyn Leighton","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/786338","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/786338"}},{"id":"786334","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"786330","description":"Horizon just in front of big cliff. Run left a couple feet off the bank","caption":"","poi_name":"Baby Canyon Doors","subject":"Chris Grindstaff","author":"Trevyn Leighton","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/786334","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/786334"}},{"id":"786337","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"786330","description":"Notice the arrow. That points to the portage. If you are on the water at that point....ouch","caption":"","poi_name":"","subject":"Twisting Falls","author":"Trevyn Leighton","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/786337","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/786337"}},{"id":"786339","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"786330","description":"Seal launch in, awesome boof off the center towards the wall. Turn left in case you get caught in the hole","caption":"","poi_name":"","subject":"Trevyn Leighton, Chris Grindstaff","author":"Edgar Peck","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/786339","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/786339"}},{"id":"786374","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"786373","description":"Gray line is approximately one stretched hand span from water level. Low, but lots of fun.","caption":"Takeout bridge Gauge","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Trevyn Leighton","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/786374","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/786374"}},{"id":"41290","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"4804","description":"Aerial Pic of Compression Falls","caption":"Aerial of Compression Falls","poi_name":"Compression Falls","subject":"","author":"Brandon Hughett","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/41290","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/41290"}},{"id":"7109","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7135","description":"","caption":"Nick at Big Falls","poi_name":"Big Falls","subject":"Nick Scott","author":"Caleb Mentzer","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/7109","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/7109"}},{"id":"7110","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7135","description":"","caption":"On the lip","poi_name":"Big Falls","subject":"Nick Scott","author":"Caleb Mentzer","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/7110","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/7110"}},{"id":"7107","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7135","description":"It's big","caption":"Caleb at the Big Falls","poi_name":"Big falls","subject":"Caleb Mentzer","author":"Nick Scott","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/7107","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/7107"}},{"id":"41293","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"4805","description":"Aerial shot of Twisting Falls","caption":"Twisting Falls Aerial","poi_name":"Twisting Falls","subject":"","author":"Brandon Hughett","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/41293","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/41293"}},{"id":"885279","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"887983","description":"This is below Compression Falls.","caption":"Dennis Huntley below the last big drop","poi_name":null,"subject":"Dennis Huntley","author":"Dennis Huntley","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/885279","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/885279"}}],"detail":{"id":"1066","permitUrl":null,"permitInfo":null,"description":"**Clay Wright contributed** on 2002-03-06 02:57:09:\n\nBig Falls (at top of this reach) has been run, with cut head and a broken back (OC-1) but several clean ones. Some flat, but good rapids, one chunky double ledge, Twisting Falls portage (narrow and high, but not scetch) then a 10-15' and a ramping 33'er which is sweet!\n\nA long shuttle is the only drawback to this cool run!\n\n**Kevin Williams added** on 2003-04-21 11:39:02\n\nAmazingly beautiful remote gorge with some truly memorable rapids!\n\nSTART EARLY to allow time for the monster shuttle (more than an hour each way) and to scout the many horizon lines, plus the twisting falls portage takes considerable time. Pay careful attention for this mandatory portage, you really don't want to miss the left eddy after the class-III drop that leads into a 15' fall and then without escape into the falls proper. Portage on the left and don't go too high - apparently there is a goat trail right along the rocks above the drop. we went straight up and ended up on a 2-hour hell hike that ended with tying 11 throw ropes together to lower the boats back to the river. Yuk!\n\nSince we accidentally portaged all the way around the falls I can't verify this but I was told that there is a nice 16' fall above a 44' fall that ends the gradient of the run. It looked clean, but by that time it was dark.\n\nMost drops are pretty straightforward but intimidating class IV and a few deserve a long look. One not too terribly heinous drop had strainers, undercuts, a sieve or two, piton rocks, a high pinning potential and ended in an undercut cave. Lots of steep slides (4') reminiscent of Swallow Falls on the Top Yough in MD. Some are stacked almost on top of one another but all have at least small pools in between. Look out for wood in the most inconvenient places and be careful of running out of eddies before drops.\n\nI can't wait to run this river again but next time I'd set shuttle the night before and camp at the campground along Big Falls Road (leads to the put-in).","averageGradient":null,"maxGradient":null,"length":"4.8","correlations":[],"pointOfInterestIDs":["112537","117824"],"geometry":{"type":"LineString","coordinates":[[-81.969131,36.199558],[-81.969124,36.199803],[-81.969167,36.199876],[-81.969262,36.200036],[-81.969391,36.200556],[-81.969246,36.201334],[-81.968549,36.202876],[-81.968592,36.203134],[-81.969387,36.203733],[-81.970034,36.203918],[-81.971663,36.